<div class="topic-container">

    <h1>checked and unchecked Keywords in C#</h1>

    <p>
        The <strong>checked</strong> and <strong>unchecked</strong> keywords in C#
        are used to control overflow checking for numeric calculations.
    </p>

    <p>
        They are mainly used with numeric data types like:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>int</li>
        <li>long</li>
        <li>short</li>
        <li>byte</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>What is Overflow?</h2>

    <p>
        Overflow happens when a value exceeds the storage capacity of a data type.
    </p>

    <p>
        Example:
    </p>

    <pre>
byte num = 255;
    </pre>

    <p>
        A <strong>byte</strong> can store values only from 0 to 255.
    </p>

    <p>
        If we increase the value by 1:
    </p>

    <pre>
num++;
    </pre>

    <p>
        The value becomes 256 which is outside the range of byte.
        This is called overflow.
    </p>

    <hr>

    <h2>checked Keyword</h2>

    <p>
        The <strong>checked</strong> keyword forces C# to throw an exception if
        overflow occurs during arithmetic operations or type conversions.
    </p>

    <h3>Example of checked</h3>

    <pre>
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        checked
        {
            byte num = 255;

            num++;

            Console.WriteLine(num);
        }
    }
}
    </pre>

    <h3>Output</h3>

    <pre>
OverflowException
    </pre>

    <p>
        Here:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>255 + 1 becomes 256</li>
        <li>byte cannot store 256</li>
        <li><strong>checked</strong> detects overflow</li>
        <li>Exception is thrown</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>unchecked Keyword</h2>

    <p>
        The <strong>unchecked</strong> keyword ignores overflow checking.
    </p>

    <p>
        Instead of throwing an exception, the value wraps around.
    </p>

    <h3>Example of unchecked</h3>

    <pre>
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        unchecked
        {
            byte num = 255;

            num++;

            Console.WriteLine(num);
        }
    }
}
    </pre>

    <h3>Output</h3>

    <pre>
0
    </pre>

    <p>
        Here:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>byte maximum value is 255</li>
        <li>After 255, value wraps around to 0</li>
        <li>No exception is thrown</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Real-world Usage</h2>

    <h3>checked</h3>

    <p>
        Used in applications where overflow can cause incorrect calculations.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Banking applications</li>
        <li>Financial calculations</li>
        <li>Tax systems</li>
        <li>Scientific calculations</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>unchecked</h3>

    <p>
        Used in performance-critical applications where overflow checking is not required.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Game development</li>
        <li>Graphics programming</li>
        <li>Low-level system operations</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Expression Syntax</h2>

    <h3>checked Expression</h3>

    <pre>
int result = checked(a + b);
    </pre>

    <h3>unchecked Expression</h3>

    <pre>
int result = unchecked(a + b);
    </pre>

    <hr>

    <h2>Difference Between checked and unchecked</h2>

    <table border="1" cellpadding="10">
        <tr>
            <th>checked</th>
            <th>unchecked</th>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Detects overflow</td>
            <td>Ignores overflow</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Throws exception</td>
            <td>No exception</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Safer</td>
            <td>Faster</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
            <td>Used in financial systems</td>
            <td>Used in performance-critical applications</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <hr>

    <h2>Important Points</h2>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>checked</strong> enables overflow checking.</li>
        <li><strong>unchecked</strong> disables overflow checking.</li>
        <li>Used only with numeric calculations and conversions.</li>
        <li>Overflow mostly occurs with small-range data types like byte and short.</li>
        <li>Can be used as blocks or expressions.</li>
    </ul>

    <hr>

    <h2>Interview Definition</h2>

    <p>
        The <strong>checked</strong> keyword in C# is used to enable overflow checking
        for numeric operations and throws an exception if overflow occurs.
        The <strong>unchecked</strong> keyword disables overflow checking and allows
        values to wrap around without throwing exceptions.
    </p>

</div>